{"id":"rotary-position-embedding-rope","t":"Rotary position embeddings, both layouts","tags":["Transformers","Attention","Embeddings"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"rope","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"RoPE encodes position by **rotating** pairs of query/key features by an angle proportional to the token's position. Pair $i$ ($i = 0, \\dots, d/2 - 1$) of a token at position $m$ is rotated by $m\\,\\theta_i$ with\n\n$$\\theta_i = \\text{base}^{-2i/d}, \\qquad \\begin{pmatrix} u' \\\\ v' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos m\\theta_i & -\\sin m\\theta_i \\\\ \\sin m\\theta_i & \\cos m\\theta_i \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} u \\\\ v \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nImplementations disagree on **which** features form pair $i$:\n\n| `layout` | pair $i$ is $(u, v) =$ |\n|---|---|\n| `'interleaved'` (original paper, GPT-J) | features $(2i,\\; 2i + 1)$ |\n| `'half'` (GPT-NeoX, Llama in Hugging Face) | features $(i,\\; i + d/2)$ |\n\nWrite `rope(x, positions, base=10000.0, layout='interleaved')`.\n\n## Shapes\n- `x`: float array `(..., T, d)` with $d$ even: the last two axes are sequence and features (leading axes such as batch and heads are left alone).\n- `positions`: int array `(T,)`, the position $m$ of each token (not necessarily $0..T-1$, e.g. for a KV cache).\n\n## Output\nFloat array shaped like `x`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef rope(x, positions, base=10000.0, layout='interleaved'):\n    \"\"\"x: (..., T, d), d even; positions: (T,). Return rotated x.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Build the angle table once: `theta = base ** (-np.arange(0, d, 2) / d)` and `ang = positions[:, None] * theta`, shape `(T, d/2)`. It broadcasts against `(..., T, d/2)`.","Pick the two halves of each pair by slicing: `x[..., 0::2]` and `x[..., 1::2]` for interleaved, `x[..., :d//2]` and `x[..., d//2:]` for half.","Rotate with $u' = u\\cos - v\\sin$, $v' = u\\sin + v\\cos$ and write the results back into the same slots they came from."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef rope(x, positions, base=10000.0, layout='interleaved'):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n    d = x.shape[-1]\n    theta = base ** (-np.arange(0, d, 2) / d)                       # (d/2,): exponent 2i/d\n    ang = np.asarray(positions, dtype=float)[:, None] * theta        # (T, d/2)\n    cos, sin = np.cos(ang), np.sin(ang)\n    if layout == 'interleaved':\n        u, v = x[..., 0::2], x[..., 1::2]\n    else:\n        u, v = x[..., : d // 2], x[..., d // 2:]\n    ru, rv = u * cos - v * sin, u * sin + v * cos                    # broadcast over leading axes\n    out = np.empty_like(x)\n    if layout == 'interleaved':\n        out[..., 0::2], out[..., 1::2] = ru, rv\n    else:\n        out[..., : d // 2], out[..., d // 2:] = ru, rv\n    return out\n","ex_md":"## Why rotations encode relative position\nRotations compose: $R(m\\theta)^\\top R(n\\theta) = R((n - m)\\theta)$. So for a query at position $m$ and a key at position $n$,\n$$\\langle R_m q, R_n k\\rangle = \\langle q, R_{n-m} k\\rangle,$$\nthe attention score depends only on the **offset** $n - m$, while the vectors themselves carry absolute positions. Unlike additive sinusoidal embeddings, nothing is added to the token content, and the rotation preserves norms.\n\n## Frequencies\n$\\theta_0 = 1$ rotates fast (a full turn every $2\\pi$ tokens), $\\theta_{d/2-1} \\approx 1/\\text{base}$ rotates very slowly. Context-extension tricks (position interpolation, NTK scaling, YaRN) all work by rescaling positions or the base so that long offsets map into the range of angles seen in training.\n\n## Layouts\nBoth layouts are the same operation on a permuted feature order. They are not interchangeable at load time: weights trained with one must be permuted (the Q/K projection rows) to work with the other, which is a real source of bugs when converting checkpoints.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(T d)$ per head, applied to queries and keys only (not values).\n\n## Common bugs\n- Using $\\theta_i = \\text{base}^{-i/d}$ (missing the factor 2).\n- Rotating with the token index $0..T-1$ instead of the given positions during cached decoding.\n- Applying RoPE to the values.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Prove the relative-position property above from the rotation matrices.\n- Why does RoPE make attention scores decay (on average) with distance?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[1., 0.],\n       [1., 0.]])\narray([0, 1])\n10000.0","out":"array([[1.        , 0.        ],\n       [0.54030231, 0.84147098]])"},{"in":"array([[1., 2., 3., 4.]])\narray([2])\n100.0\n'half'","out":"array([[-3.14403912,  1.16545583, -0.33914308,  4.31760497]])"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ai-engineering-course/llm-internals/positional-embeddings/","t":"Positional embeddings","c":"AI Engineering"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/inference-eng-course/triton-kernel-engineering/write-rope-fused-operators/","t":"Write RoPE & Fused Operators","c":"Inference Engineering"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/deep-learning/sequence-models/transformers-for-sequences/","t":"Transformers for sequences","c":"Deep Learning"}],"related":["sinusoidal-positional-encoding","scaled-dot-product-attention","multi-head-attention"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [1.0, 0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0, 1], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, 10000.0], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.5403023058681398, 0.8414709848078965]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, 100.0, \"half\"], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[-3.1440391170241875, 1.1654558325022384, -0.33914308281574557, 4.317604972955089]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-1.4669297460589656, -0.41963809931961565, -0.04056875054436116, 0.3927974448412752, -1.0346470820947515, 1.1873383101711537, 0.21748802571811543, -0.5326509961907064], [-1.5284289838743144, -0.6693935462283623, 0.7003655997792447, 0.5437365521307904, 0.88479